#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952} :activity/id #uuid "6fbac718-0db1-5d0a-8eac-581af3c3ed39" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001 :topic/id #uuid "789ccc3c-cca0-5e71-bdb6-b757b2abcfa0" :topic/math-academy-id 3001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163262 :tutorial/content "We often want to know if there is a relationship between two random variables. For this reason, we wish to formulate the idea of a joint probability distribution.\n\nTo introduce this concept, suppose the random variable $X$ represents the number of heads obtained when a fair coin is flipped. The support of $X,$ denoted $S_X,$ consists of all possible values of $X$ and in this case is given by\n\n$$\nS_X = \\{0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow suppose that the random variable $Y$ represents the result of rolling a fair tetrahedral die. Then the support of $Y,$ denoted $S_Y,$ is\n\n$$\nS_Y = \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe **joint support** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $S,$ consists of all possible pairs $(x,y)$ such that $x$ is a possible outcome for $X$ and $y$ is a possible outcome for $Y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0,1), & (0,2), & (0,3), & (0,4) \\\\ (1,1), & (1,2), & (1,3), & (1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $S_X\\times S_Y$ is the cartesian product of $S_X$ and $S_Y.$\n\nA **joint probability distribution** is a probability distribution over a set of pairs [math](X, Y),[/math] where [math]X[/math] and [math]Y[/math] are both random variables. In this lesson, we will focus on the case where [math]X[/math] and [math]Y[/math] are *discrete* random variables, in which case the joint probability distribution is called a **joint probability mass function**. \n\nLet's assume that the flipping of the coin and rolling of the die are independent. Then, for any $x \\in X$ and any $y \\in Y,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\:\\textrm{and}\\:Y=y) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe **joint probability mass function** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $f(x,y),$ can be represented by the following table:\n\n| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSimilar to the case of single random variables, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).\n$$\n\nGoing forward, we will use the shorthand notation $P(X=x, Y=y)$ to mean $P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79b99576-5a28-5c03-bc5d-1cc472ef0795" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5348 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9628dc39-75f0-5169-a945-48c68e6e4f31" :step/math-academy-id 33477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161863 :question/id #uuid "2d698f44-e551-58a2-883b-3a992119bd76" :question/math-academy-id "e-9126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275145}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "3e1d6fef-61c1-5a3a-805d-6a020df8bb08" :step/math-academy-id 34016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162073 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161873 :question/id #uuid "f2789f6c-11d5-5132-91b3-be65dac4ce24" :question/math-academy-id "e-9203"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e20c64-85be-5cb5-acde-17ffd8b6aff5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423159}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "b093ac87-86c2-5281-860f-df1321b270d4" :step/math-academy-id 34339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162074 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161874 :question/id #uuid "01b86f31-f31e-567d-b798-fc0f10a4d0b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9204"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e247e2c9-3e47-5cf4-be8d-f1f22bb30b39" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247204}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability Containing Inequalities From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "657c560d-1a7e-5600-8883-4eaa35b157cd" :step/math-academy-id 34343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163328 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively. For a function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=P(X=x, Y=y)\n$$\n\n \n\nto be a valid joint probability mass function with joint support $S=S_X\\times S_Y,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y) \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y)= 1$\n\n- $P\\left((X,Y) \\in A \\right) = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in A} f(x,y),$ where $A$ is a subset of $S.$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to these conditions.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y)\\in S$ states that any possible outcome for $(X,Y)$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1.$\n\n- The condition $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y) = 1$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ and $Y$ must add up to $1.$\n\n- The third condition states that we compute the probability of an event $A$ by adding up the probabilities associated with $A.$\n\nThe joint probability mass function of a discrete random variable with finite support can be represented using a table, as shown below.\n\n|  | $Y=y_1$ | $Y=y_2$ | $\\ldots$ | $Y=y_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=x_1$ | $f(x_1,y_1)$ | $f(x_1,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_1,y_n)$ |\n| $X=x_2$ | $f(x_2,y_1)$ | $f(x_2,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_2,y_n)$ |\n| $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ |\n| $X=x_k$ | $f(x_k,y_1)$ | $f(x_k,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_k,y_n)$ |\n\nIn the case of two random variables, the joint distribution is sometimes called a **bivariate distribution**.\nHowever, the concept generalizes to any number of random variables, giving a **multivariate distribution**." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f5858f9-f1f3-5d98-b436-9ffd034a3e9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6284 :tutorial/title "Bivariate and Multivariate Distributions"} :step/id #uuid "2636297d-b114-590c-9454-08ebab970ce2" :step/math-academy-id 38084 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982}}] :activity/title "Joint Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162162}]}
